From: Ruohang Feng <rh@vonng.com>
Date: Sun, 2 Aug 2026 01:45:00 +0800
Subject: Fix zero-radius GEOSEARCH rounding

Origin: vendor
Bug: https://github.com/valkey-io/valkey/issues/2051
Forwarded: no
Last-Update: 2026-08-02

Redis 7.2 and Valkey share this geospatial distance implementation. Compiler
optimizations can leave tiny rounding errors in both the longitude comparison
and the latitude conversion. Backport the Valkey near-zero longitude
comparison and return the mathematically exact result for identical latitude
inputs so a zero-radius GEOSEARCH includes its source member.
---
 src/geohash_helper.c | 8 +++++---
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/geohash_helper.c b/src/geohash_helper.c
--- a/src/geohash_helper.c
+++ b/src/geohash_helper.c
@@ -224,14 +224,16 @@ double geohashGetLatDistance(double lat1d, double lat2d) {
 double geohashGetLatDistance(double lat1d, double lat2d) {
+    if (lat1d == lat2d) return 0.0;
     return EARTH_RADIUS_IN_METERS * fabs(deg_rad(lat2d) - deg_rad(lat1d));
 }
-
+
 /* Calculate distance using haversine great circle distance formula. */
 double geohashGetDistance(double lon1d, double lat1d, double lon2d, double lat2d) {
     double lat1r, lon1r, lat2r, lon2r, u, v, a;
     lon1r = deg_rad(lon1d);
     lon2r = deg_rad(lon2d);
     v = sin((lon2r - lon1r) / 2);
-    /* if v == 0 we can avoid doing expensive math when lons are practically the same */
-    if (v == 0.0)
+    const double GEO_EPSILON = 1e-15;
+    /* Avoid expensive math when longitudes are practically the same. */
+    if (fabs(v) <= GEO_EPSILON)
         return geohashGetLatDistance(lat1d, lat2d);
     lat1r = deg_rad(lat1d);
